\f1\fs22 The Chuglinghams\'92 barbecue wound down as the moon rested on Narrow Neck Plateau separating Jamison Valley from Megalong Valley farther west. Hayley invited Kathy for a walk down to the lookout. Together they watched the last splash of sunset colours fade from the sky. The awkwardness that had shadowed them throughout the afternoon persisted, exposed in the vast open space before them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93So,\'94 Hayley said as she cast her vision far into the valley, \'93stasis technology. Makes you wonder what else they\'92re keeping from us.\'94\
Kathy raised an eyebrow. \'93Still on that?\'94 She wasn\'92t sure whether to close the distance between them or accept the offer of more work talk.\
\'93Sorry, occupational hazard.\'94 Hayley leaned against the safety railing. \'93Old cop habits. Can\'92t stop questioning things.\'94 She adjusted her weight on her feet and somehow finished a few centimetres closer to Kathy.\
\'93That\'92s what makes you\'85\'94 Kathy trailed off with a half-smile then looked away.\
\'93Makes me what?\'94\
Kathy almost regretted not putting on a fresh patch, but she wanted to feel\'85 this. \'93Oh, I dunno.\'94 She shrugged and leaned on the railing too.\
Receiving neither compliment nor criticism, Hayley stared away to the south. \'93How\'92s Sarah?\'94 she asked after a few stretched moments.\
\'93Patient confidentiality,\'94 Kathy replied automatically, then sighed. \'93Her pregnancy is... progressing.\'94\
\'93That\'92s specific.\'94\
\'93It\'92s complicated. She\'92s at once the most exhausted and most radiant expectant mother I\'92ve ever seen, and that\'92s just according to her charts.\'94\
\'93She is radiant,\'94 Hayley agreed and Kathy felt a pang of jealousy. Hayley added, \'93Nothing ever seems to slow her down. I mean, she fought off a professional assassin in an elevator, for tax sake. On her wedding day.\'94\
\'93While carrying a some genetically engineered version of a long-dead\'85\'94 Kathy shook her head, aching for a patch.\
\'93Probably nothing any expectant mother wouldn\'92t be capable of,\'94 Hayley said.\
Kathy stood back from the fence and drummed the railing. \'93Ah, yeah, motherhood, eh? Not something I\'92d ever be cut out for.\'94\
Hayley tried to meet Kathy\'92s gaze, but she assiduously avoided it. \'93I think you underestimate yourself.\'94\
\'93Me?\'94 Kathy blinked the notion away. \'93Nah. Fast times and fun times are all I\'92m good for.\'94 She gulped and became very still. \'93In the end, I\'92ll always be\'97\'94 A visceral reaction helped her cut off the last of her thought, and she cursed herself for always putting obstacles in the way of those who drew too close.\
Hayley pushed her weight off the safety rail and turned for Kathy. \'93Well fast times and fun times are good times too.\'94 She reached for Kathy\'92s elbow. \'93We could start there first.\'94\
For the first time, Kathy met Hayley\'92s gaze. Where she\'92d expected to see the steely eyes of a professional investigator she saw only the starlight and mist in softening eyes. She saw a perfect arch of an eyebrow, the curve of a nose line designed by an artist, the smoothest, firmest, most sensual cheek, and the most minimal smile that spoke of the forgiveness of all her sins.\
\'93I\'92ve been thinking about you since the day we met,\'94 Hayley confessed and caressed Kathy\'92s cheek.\
Kathy had been thinking in similar fashion about Sarah since the day they\'92d met but felt her resistance dissolving under Hayley\'92s touch. \'93Tax! Let\'92s roll with it.\'94\
They embraced and kissed. In moments, Hayley was breathless and wide-eyed. At the first opportunity, Kathy thrust her hand under Hayley\'92s blouse.\
\'93Ouch!\'94 Hayley cried, and smacked Kathy\'92s hand away. \'93Typical doctor. Where\'92s your bedside manner?\'94\
\'93I save it for when I\'92m bed-side.\'94\
\'93Oh, I like the sound of that.\'94\
Kathy grabbed Hayley\'92s hand, and led her up the lookout path. The lampposts seemed to conspire in their favour, lighting the way back to Kathy\'92s townhouse with uncharacteristic cheerfulness.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Kathy\'92s bedroom door suffered the indignity of being flung open with such enthusiasm it bounced off the wall and nearly closed itself again in protest. The two tumbled onto the bed like a pair of acrobats who\'92d forgotten the rest of their routine and decided to improvise anyway.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Wait,\'94 Hayley gasped between kisses, \'93are those glow-in-the-dark star stickers on your ceiling?\'94\
\'93Focus, detective,\'94 Kathy murmured, working on Hayley\'92s buttons with the dexterity of someone who\'92d spent years tying surgical knots. \'93You can investigate my decor later.\'94\
Hayley\'92s police-issue practicality met Kathy\'92s medical-professional sensibility in what followed\'97a convergence of two people who spent their days dealing with complexity and chaos, and had apparently decided that tonight called for a different kind of empirical research.\
\'93You know,\'94 Hayley said, tracing a finger along Kathy\'92s collarbone, \'93for someone who claims to be all about fast times, you\'92re remarkably thorough.\'94\
\'93Medical training,\'94 Kathy replied, then performed something extremely examinatory.\
Hayley melted.\
The bedside lamp didn\'92t survive their enthusiasm intact\'97knocked over once, then again for good measure. Neither did the neat arrangement of medical journals on Kathy\'92s nightstand, which joined the growing archipelago of discarded clothing across the floor. Kathy\'92s exhortative language would have made a drill sergeant blush, but Hayley seemed to find it motivating.\
Later, as they lay tangled in sheets that looked and felt like they\'92d been through a category three cyclone, Hayley traced lazy circles on Kathy\'92s shoulder. \'93So, doctor, will I live?\'94\
Kathy grinned. \'93Uncertain. We might need a few follow-up examinations. Some tests. More procedures, with  strange apparatus.\'94\
Outside, the night wrapped around them like a discreet curtain. Somewhere close, perhaps in the trees by the back fence, a riot of kookaburras laughed as if they knew exactly what kind of fast and fun times had just transpired.
